            <ArticleTitle>Neuroanatomical substrate of noise sensitivity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>309-315</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1053-8119(17)30973-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.neuroimage.2017.11.041</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recent functional studies suggest that noise sensitivity, a trait describing attitudes towards noise and predicting noise annoyance, is associated with altered processing in the central auditory system. In the present work, we examined whether noise sensitivity could be related to the structural anatomy of auditory and limbic brain areas. Anatomical MR brain images of 80 subjects were parcellated with FreeSurfer to measure grey matter volume, cortical thickness, cortical area and folding index of anatomical structures in the temporal lobe and insular cortex. The grey matter volume of amygdala and hippocampus was measured as well. According to our findings, noise sensitivity is associated with the grey matter volume in the selected structures. Among those, we propose and discuss particular areas, previously linked to auditory perceptual, emotional and interoceptive processing, in which larger grey matter volume seems to be related to higher noise sensitivity.</AbstractText>
